 "_comment": "Hero image-sequence experiment, source flow2d (scripts/prepare-sequence.mjs): the frames of each take used, chosen by the clean-frame rules of scripts/sequence_report.py to cover the cursor's pose field on a 1.25 degree lattice, from clip 2's two level legs outward (experiments/hero-sequence/NEW-TAKES-AUDIT.md, rounds 3 and 4). Takes: clip 2 (the level sweep), R-up-1 and R-up-2 (up and upper diagonals), N4 (round 4's nod: down at centre, up and up-left; it replaces round 3's V-2). Runs are stretches of a take close in time. Shifts are rigid, in crop pixels at 1080p: clip 2's late leg onto its early one (measured at their join, frames 204 and 16), and every other take onto clip 2 (each take's rest frame against clip 2's frame 16). Clip 2's middle run is left out so a level sweep stays on its two legs. Where takes overlap, N4 owns the viewer's upper left, except R-up's row at 5 to 7 degrees up there, the only frames between the level turn and N4's hold at 10 to 14. R5 (round 5, pinned from clip 2 frame 26 to R-up-2 frame 51) adds the up row about 12 degrees up from 8 degrees left across to 12.7 right, the only real up-right footage; its colour gain brings its warmer skin to clip 2's. N4's frames beyond 11 degrees left (118-157) are left out: with them the top edge alternated between N4 and R-up-1's row; R-up-1 owns the far upper left. U6 (round 6, start and end pinned to clip 2's rest frame) owns straight up: a look up to 10-12 degrees within 2 degrees of centre, brows lifted and eyes wide (102-111% of rest). The hooded straight-up frames it replaces (R-up-2's centre column, R5's row centre, N4's return near centre) are left out, except the level turn's last frame on each side, which covers production's full level reach. Round 7 (FLOW-PROMPTS.md, 'second try'): R7 is the mirrored up-right take, flipped back (…192458-flipped.mp4, made with ffmpeg hflip): up-right at yaw -12 to -20 with eyes open (95-140%); R7b is the upper arc (…192508), used only where it sits slightly up near centre and to the right (frames 28-40, 45-56; 41-44 blink). Its head drifts left as it sweeps (9 to 21 px against clip 2 at the same yaw, at little pitch or roll), so each R7b frame has its own shift.. Both replace R-up-2 and R5, whose up-right frames were hooded (74-80%). Round 8: D8 is the lower arc (…193316, FLOW-PROMPTS.md Round 7 B), frames 102-146: chin 3-7 degrees down, eyes on the lens (83-109%), sweeping from -21 to +20 degrees yaw; its start matches clip 2 frame 26 to -0.5, +0.2 px. On the viewer's left its head sits up to 7 px right of clip 2's at the same yaw, so each frame has its own shift. Placement (round 8): N4, U6 and D8 slide sideways as they move (N4 +19 to -14 plate px against clip 2 at the same yaw, U6 +6 to -12, with little roll), so each of their frames is shifted onto clip 2 face position at that yaw. R7 and R-up-1 were first left alone, taking their offsets for roll (2-7 degrees), but image alignment at the cuts showed them 7-15 plate px out too (round 9), so they are placed the same way. Frames the page can never show (turned beyond its 15.8 degrees, or up beyond its 9.3, or always beaten; found by replaying a 201x201 grid of pointer positions and 64 radial paths) are left out, except the level turn's last frame on each side, which covers production's full level reach. Tone (round 9): every frame outside clip 2 has its own colour gain, matching its skin (cheeks and forehead, around the face) to clip 2's at the same yaw, smoothed over neighbouring frames; the takes had differed by up to 3 levels (U6 cooler, N4 and D8 warmer). Density (round 9): R7b and D8 use every source frame in their ranges (they had been sampled every 2-4 frames, 2-3.5 degree steps); the added frames take their shift and gain from their neighbours.",
